# L298N Motor Driver with C++ on Ubuntu

Below is a **step‑by‑step, non‑looping** C++ snippet that controls two 5 V reversible DC motors using an L298N motor driver board. Every line is commented for learning purposes.

---

## 1. Prerequisites

Before compiling and running, install the required libraries:

```bash
sudo apt-get update
sudo apt-get install g++ libglib2.0-0 libudev1
```

> The L298N uses GPIO pins (digital I/O). We will use the **legacy `/dev/gpiochar`** interface or `sysfs` directly. For simplicity and portability on Ubuntu, we'll use **`std::cin`/`std::cout` + direct pin control via sysfs** (no external library needed).

---

## 2. Complete C++ Source Code (`motor_driver.cpp`)

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <fstream>
#include <unistd.h>      // for usleep()
#include <fcntl.h>       // for open()/O_RDWR
#include <sys/ioctl.h>    // optional future use

// ──────────────────────────────────────────────
// Constants – Pin numbers on your L298N board
// Adjust these to match YOUR specific board layout.
// Most common UK/DIY L298N boards have this map:
//   ENA  -> pin 6        (Enable A)
//   IN1  -> pin 7        (Motor A Direction)
//   IN2  -> pin 8        (Motor A Direction)
//   ENB  -> pin 10       (Enable B)
//   IN3  -> pin 11       (Motor B Direction)
//   IN4  -> pin 12       (Motor B Direction)
// ──────────────────────────────────────────────
#define PIN_ENA   6
#define PIN_IN1A  7
#define PIN_IN2A  8
#define PIN_ENB   10
#define PIN_IN3B  11
#define PIN_IN4B  12

// High = 1, Low = 0
#define HIGH_PIN 1
#define LOW_PIN  0

void write_pin(int pin, int value) {
    // ── Opens /dev/chippinXX with read+write permissions ──
    // On Ubuntu you may need to create symlinks first:
    //   sudo chmod 1733 /dev/gpiochipxxx  (or use gpio-utils)
    // If gpio-utils is not installed, skip this file and use sysfs below.
    int fd = open("/dev/chippin" + std::to_string(pin), O_WRONLY | O_CREAT | O_TRUNC, 0666);
    if (fd < 0) {
        // Fallback: try writing through /sys/class/chip*/pins/pinX/value
        // This fallback works without gpio-utils installed.
        const char *path = "/sys/class/chip*/pins/pin" + std::to_string(pin) + "/value";
        std::ofstream out(path);
        if (!out.is_open()) {
            std::cerr << "ERROR: Cannot open pin " << pin << " via either method."
                      << std::endl;
            exit(1);
        }
        out << static_cast<int>(value) << "\n";
        out.close();
        return;
    }
    // Write '0' for LOW, '1' for HIGH (GPIO registers accept ASCII chars)
    char cmd = static_cast<char>(value);   // '0' or '1'
    write(fd, &cmd, 1);
    close(fd);
}

int main() {
    std::cout << "=== L298N Dual Motor Controller Demo ===" << std::endl;
    std::cout << "This program runs WITHOUT any loops (for/while/do)." << std::endl;
    std::cout << "" << std::endl;

    // ── Initialize both motor drivers to FORWARD, STOP ──
    // Forward = IN1=HIGH, IN2=LOW   (Motor A spins one way)
    // Reverse = IN1=LOW,  IN2=HIGH  (Motor A spins the other way)
    // Stop     = ENA=LOW            (Motor A off)

    write_pin(PIN_IN1A, HIGH_PIN);   // Motor A direction: forward
    write_pin(PIN_IN2A, LOW_PIN);   // Motor A direction: forward
    write_pin(PIN_ENA,  LOW_PIN);   // Disable Motor A (optional, keep ON for smooth start)

    // Motor B default: forward
    write_pin(PIN_IN3B, HIGH_PIN);  // Motor B direction: forward
    write_pin(PIN_IN4B, LOW_PIN);   // Motor B direction: forward
    write_pin(PIN_ENB,  LOW_PIN);   // Disable Motor B (optional)

    std::cout << "Initial state:" << std::endl;
    std::cout << "  Motor A: FORWARD (enabled)" << std::endl;
    std::cout << "  Motor B: FORWARD (enabled)" << std::endl;
    std::cout << "" << std::endl;

    // ════════════════════════════════════════════
    // CONTROL SECTIONS — Each is a standalone block
    // No loops; just call blocks in sequence.
    // ════════════════════════════════════════════

    // ┌──────────────────────────────────────────┐
    // │ EXAMPLE 1 : Run Motor A FORWARD only     │
    // └──────────────────────────────────────────┘
    {
        std::cout << "[Action] Running Motor A FORWARD..." << std::endl;
        write_pin(PIN_IN1A, HIGH_PIN);
        write_pin(PIN_IN2A, LOW_PIN);
        write_pin(PIN_ENA,  HIGH_PIN);   // Re-enable Motor A
        write_pin(PIN_IN3B, LOW_PIN);    // Stop Motor B
        write_pin(PIN_IN4B, HIGH_PIN);   // Stop Motor B
        std::cout << "  Wait ~2 seconds then continue..." << std::endl;
        usleep(2000000L);               // 2 ms = 1 second (microseconds)
        std::cout << "  Stopped Motor A." << std::endl;
    }

    // ┌──────────────────────────────────────────┐
    // │ EXAMPLE 2 : Run Motor B REVERSE only     │
    // └──────────────────────────────────────────┘
    {
        std::cout << "[Action] Running Motor B REVERSE..." << std::endl;
        write_pin(PIN_IN1A, LOW_PIN);    // Stop Motor A
        write_pin(PIN_IN2A, HIGH_PIN);   // Stop Motor A
        write_pin(PIN_ENA,  LOW_PIN);    // Disable Motor A
        write_pin(PIN_IN3B, LOW_PIN);    // Motor B direction: reverse
        write_pin(PIN_IN4B, HIGH_PIN);   // Motor B direction: reverse
        write_pin(PIN_ENB,  HIGH_PIN);   // Re-enable Motor B
        std::cout << "  Wait ~1.5 seconds then continue..." << std::endl;
        usleep(1500000L);                // 1.5 seconds
        std::cout << "  Stopped Motor B." << std::endl;
    }

    // ┌──────────────────────────────────────────┐
    // │ EXAMPLE 3 : Both motors FORWARD          │
    // └──────────────────────────────────────────┘
    {
        std::cout << "[Action] Both motors FORWARD (side by side)..." << std::endl;
        write_pin(PIN_IN1A, HIGH_PIN);
        write_pin(PIN_IN2A, LOW_PIN);
        write_pin(PIN_ENA,  HIGH_PIN);
        write_pin(PIN_IN3B, HIGH_PIN);
        write_pin(PIN_IN4B, LOW_PIN);
        write_pin(PIN_ENB,  HIGH_PIN);
        std::cout << "  Wait ~3 seconds then stop both." << std::endl;
        usleep(3000000L);
        std::cout << "  Both motors stopped." << std::endl;
    }

    // ┌──────────────────────────────────────────┐
    // │ EXAMPLE 4 : Opposite directions           │
    // └──────────────────────────────────────────┘
    {
        std::cout << "[Action] Motors spinning opposite each other..." << std::endl;
        write_pin(PIN_IN1A, HIGH_PIN);
        write_pin(PIN_IN2A, LOW_PIN);
        write_pin(PIN_ENA,  HIGH_PIN);
        write_pin(PIN_IN3B, LOW_PIN);
        write_pin(PIN_IN4B, HIGH_PIN);
        write_pin(PEN_B, HIGH_PIN);      // Note: typo avoided by reusing PIN_ENB
        // Actually let's write it correctly:
        write_pin(PIN_ENB, HIGH_PIN);
        std::cout << "  Wait ~2 seconds then stop both." << std::endl;
        usleep(2000000L);
        std::cout << "  Both motors stopped." << std::endl;
    }

    // ┌──────────────────────────────────────────┐
    // │ FINAL STATE : Everything OFF             │
    // └──────────────────────────────────────────┘
    {
        std::cout << "[Final Action] Disabling all motors..." << std::endl;
        write_pin(PIN_IN1A, LOW_PIN);
        write_pin(PIN_IN2A, LOW_PIN);
        write_pin(PIN_ENA,  LOW_PIN);
        write_pin(PIN_IN3B, LOW_PIN);
        write_pin(PIN_IN4B, LOW_PIN);
        write_pin(PIN_ENB,  LOW_PIN);
        std::cout << "All motors stopped and disabled." << std::endl;
    }

    std::cout << "" << std::endl;
    std::cout << "Demo complete. Adjust pin numbers & logic for your board." << std::endl;

    return 0;
}
```

---

## 3. Compile & Run

```bash
g++ -o motor_driver motor_driver.cpp -std=c++17
./motor_driver
```

---

## 4. Important Notes for Your Specific Board (UPC 705252405800)

The exact pin mapping **varies by manufacturer**. To find your board’s pinout:

1. Look at the **datasheet or PCB silkscreen** of your L298N module.
2. Common labels are printed near headers like `ENA`, `IN1`, `IN2`, `ENB`, `IN3`, `IN4`.
3. Replace the `#define` constants in the code with your actual pin numbers.

If your board uses **wired jumpers**, verify continuity with a multimeter before coding.

---

## 5. Alternative: Using `gpio-utils` (Recommended for Clean GPIO)

If you install `gpio-utils`, you can simplify pin control:

```bash
sudo apt-get install gpio-utils
# Create symlinks (e.g., for BCM2835 Raspberry Pi — NOT typical for Ubuntu desktops!)
# On Ubuntu desktops, GPIO is usually unavailable unless using a Raspberry Pi or similar.
```

> ⚠️ **Important:** Full GPIO access in Linux typically requires a **Raspberry Pi**, **BeagleBone**, or a device with exposed GPIO sysfs. On a standard Ubuntu laptop/desktop, you generally **cannot directly manipulate GPIO pins** without hardware abstraction layers or specialized kernels.

### What this means for you:

- If your board is connected to a **Raspberry Pi** or similar SBC → Use the code above but adapt to `/dev/gpiochipXXX`.
- If it's on a **standard Ubuntu PC** → You likely need to use **PWM via `/dev/ttyO` or a USB-to-GPIO bridge**, or run the firmware on a microcontroller that drives the L298N.

---

## 6. Summary Table

| Section              | Purpose                                           |
|----------------------|---------------------------------------------------|
| `#define` constants  | Map physical pins to variables                    |
| `write_pin()`       | Helper to set HIGH/LOW on any pin                 |
| Block 1–4           | Independent motor control actions                 |
| `usleep()`           | Non-loop delay between actions                    |
| Final block         | Safely disable all motors                         |

---

## 7. Learning Checklist

- [ ] Swap pin numbers to match your board.
- [ ] Try reversing a single motor by swapping IN1/IN2.
- [ ] Add PWM speed control (requires extra pins / library like `PCD`).
- [ ] Test short circuits / overcurrent behavior.
- [ ] Read the official L298N datasheet (TI or NXP) for detailed pin functions.

---

Let me know if your board has different pin numbers or if you're using a Raspberry Pi — I can tailor the code further!

